A finger held still is a long press, as on the rest of the phone.

USER 2026-10-01, after using the desktop on the phone: "the long-tap
vs scroll distinction is too sensitive to scroll's advantage". AndroWish
turns a finger held for a second into the right button (Tk's
<Button-3>, the desktop's context menus), but any motion at all after
the first 100 ms cancelled it, and a finger held on a phone's screen is
never still: it drifts as it presses, so a long press scrolled instead.

Now the finger may wander up to twice the device's touch slop from
where it went down (Android's own tolerance for a long press) before it
is a drag, and the window does not move under it until then. It is held
for the device's long press timeout (the user's "touch and hold delay"),
not a fixed second. DesktopActivity puts both in the environment
(SDLTK_TOUCH_SLOP, SDLTK_LONGPRESS_MS) before Tk starts; without them,
AndroWish's own "nearby" distance and 500 ms.

Once the long press has fired, the finger drags the right button:
motion is <B3-Motion> until it lifts, where it used to release the
right button and press the left. The desktop selects text that way
(lib/android.tcl).

